NAME
       restorecon - set files back to their default SELinux contexts

SYNOPSIS
       restorecon [-R] [-v] [-F] [-n] [-i] [-e DIRECTORY] PATH...
       restorecon [-R] [-v] -f FILE

DESCRIPTION
       Every file on an SELinux system has a context (label), such as
       system_u:object_r:httpd_sys_content_t:s0. The policy has a list of
       the context each path should have; restorecon looks each given path
       up in that list and changes the file's context to match.

       The list comes from the policy plus your own rules made with
       "semanage fcontext -a". restorecon never reads those rules from
       anywhere else, which makes the pair the standard way to label files
       for a service:

           semanage fcontext -a -t TYPE '/path(/.*)?'
           restorecon -Rv /path

       Use restorecon after moving files with mv (mv keeps the old label,
       cp gives a new one), after chcon experiments, and when an AVC denial
       in the audit log shows a file with the wrong type.

       By default restorecon changes only the type part of a context (the
       third field); -F resets the user, role and level too.

       Only root can run it. On a system with SELinux disabled it does
       nothing.

OPTIONS
       -R, -r Recurse into directories.

       -v     Show every file whose context changes ("Relabeled ... from
              ... to ...").

       -F     Force: reset the whole context, not only the type.

       -n     Do not change anything; with -v, show what would change.

       -i     Ignore files that do not exist.

       -e DIRECTORY
              Leave this directory out (may be given more than once).

       -f FILE
              Read the paths to fix from FILE, one per line.

       -p     Show progress (a count of files).

EXAMPLES
       Fix the labels of a web site copied into /var/www/html:

           restorecon -Rv /var/www/html

       Serve files from /srv/web with Apache:

           semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_content_t '/srv/web(/.*)?'
           restorecon -Rv /srv/web
           ls -Zd /srv/web

       See what would change in /home/student without changing it:

           restorecon -Rnv /home/student

       Check one file's current and expected context:

           ls -Z /var/www/html/index.html
           matchpathcon /var/www/html/index.html

NOTES
       To relabel the whole file system at the next boot (for example after
       resetting the root password in rd.break), create the file
       /.autorelabel, or run fixfiles onboot.

       A context set with chcon is undone by restorecon (and by a full
       relabel). Use semanage fcontext for changes that must last.

FILES
       /etc/selinux/targeted/contexts/files/file_contexts
              The policy's default contexts.

       /etc/selinux/targeted/contexts/files/file_contexts.local
              The rules added with semanage fcontext.

SEE ALSO
       semanage(8), chcon(1), ls(1), matchpathcon(8), setfiles(8),
       fixfiles(8), selinux(8)
